  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The demonstration relies on hypothetical 5% modal shift scenarios in a single city to illustrate the model's uncertainty framework rather than evaluating empirical policy interventions.",
      "what": "This study demonstrates the uncertainty and Value of Information (VoI) analysis features of the ITHIM-Global model using Bogotá as a case study. The model simulates the health impacts of three hypothetical scenarios—each representing a 5% increase in cycling, public transport, or car journeys—across pathways of air pollution, physical activity, and traffic fatalities. The analysis shows that cycling and transit increases consistently yield positive health outcomes despite input uncertainties, while car increases consistently yield negative outcomes, and that perfect knowledge of individual input parameters could reduce output standard deviation by up to 7%.",
      "why": "It provides a method for urban health planners to quantify how input data uncertainties affect health impact projections, identifying which specific data points are most critical to measure accurately to improve decision-making confidence."
    }
  },
